In Spring Framework AOP, a Proxy is an intermediate object that sits between the client and the actual object (target bean)
and intercepts method calls.

Think of it as a middleman.

1️⃣ Simple Definition

A Proxy is a wrapper object created by Spring that intercepts method calls to add extra behavior
(like logging, security, transactions) before or after the real method runs.

2️⃣ Simple Real-Life Example

Imagine you want to talk to a CEO.

You usually cannot talk directly.
Instead you talk to the assistant.

Client → Assistant → CEO

The assistant can:

check permission

record meeting

schedule time

In programming:

Client → Proxy → Real Object

The Proxy can:

log method calls

check security

start transactions

measure performance

3️⃣ Example Without Proxy
public class UserService {

    public void createUser() {
        System.out.println("User created");
    }
}

Client call:

userService.createUser();

Flow:

Client → UserService

No extra behavior.

4️⃣ Example With Proxy (Spring AOP)

Spring creates a proxy around the bean.

Client → Proxy → UserService

Flow:

1️⃣ Client calls method
2️⃣ Proxy intercepts the call
3️⃣ Proxy executes aspect logic
4️⃣ Proxy calls real method

Example:

Client
 ↓
Proxy (logging)
 ↓
UserService.createUser()

Output:

Method called
User created
5️⃣ Example in Spring Boot

Aspect:

@Aspect
@Component
public class LoggingAspect {

    @Before("execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))")
    public void log() {
        System.out.println("Method called");
    }
}

Spring internally creates:

UserServiceProxy

So when client calls:

userService.createUser();

Actual flow:

Client
 ↓
UserServiceProxy
 ↓
LoggingAspect
 ↓
UserService
6️⃣ Why Proxy is Important in Spring

Spring uses proxies for many features:

AOP (logging, security)

Transactions (@Transactional)

Security (@PreAuthorize)

Lazy loading

🔑 Interview One-Line Answer

A proxy is an object created by Spring that wraps the target bean and intercepts method calls to apply additional behavior
 like logging, security, or transactions before or after the actual method execution.

